window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-03-1", "source": "2023年03月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose you have taken part in a career planning seminar. Write an account of the seminar and summarize what you have learned from it. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Last week, our university held a career planning seminar in the lecture hall, which attracted a large number of\nstudents who are about to face job hunting. The lecture was delivered by an experienced human resources\nmanager, who has helped many graduates find suitable jobs.\nDuring the seminar, the speaker focused on how to plan a career and pointed out that many graduates feel lost\nsimply because they have never planned ahead. He suggested that we should first recognize our own\ninterests and strengths, then set clear and realistic goals, and finally start to build up relevant skills as early\nas possible. What impressed me most was a real story about a student who got an ideal offer through years of\ncareful preparation.\nThis seminar made me realize that career planning should never be put off until graduation. From now on, I\nwill think seriously about what I really want and take small steps toward my goals instead of drifting along.\nAfter all, a well-planned future begins with action today."}, "translation": {"prompt": "随着生活水平的提高，更多人开始加入到自驾游的行列之中。自驾游者既可驾驶自家车也可借车或租车出游。司机可能是车主或结伴出游者。自驾游与传统的组团旅游不同，它能够更好地满足旅游者的个性化需求，使他们更好地享受旅游的过程。自驾游尤其受到年轻出游者的欢迎。年轻人追求独立自由的生活，而自驾游恰好满足了他们的这一需求。", "sample": "With the improvement of living standards, more and more people are joining self-driving tours. A self-driving tourist can either drive their own car or borrow or rent one for the trip, and the driver may be the car owner or a travel companion. Unlike traditional group tours, self-driving tours can better satisfy tourists' personalized needs and thus help them enjoy the travel experience more fully. They are especially popular among young travelers. Young people pursue an independent and free life, while self- driving tours happen to meet this very need."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2023-03-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It's a fantasy that goes back centuries: a message in a bottle, carried ashore from far-off lands. Authors, artists and children  __26__  have dreamed of such a gift from the sea.\nThis time, though, it's not a bottle that washes ashore. It's eggs—thousands of little toy eggs.\nThat's what happened on the German island of Langeoog this week.\nLying just off the North Sea coast, it found itself  __27__  by an invasion of colored plastic eggs—much to the  __28__  of local children, because the eggs contained toys.\nPolice  __29__  the eggs came from a freighter that lost part of its cargo during an unusually  __30__  storm, the worst to hit Germany's northeastern coast since 2006.\nAt any rate, what was lost has now been found by many of the community's littlest residents.\n\"The surprise eggs have found their way to freedom,\" said Mayor Uwe Garrels. However, the joy of the moment  __31__  off soon.\n\"At first I thought this was a wonder, because everything was so  __32__  , but then we realized that this is a huge  __33__  in the end,\" said the mayor. He also noted the plastic bags and other materials that have washed ashore on the island can cause serious problems for  __34__  .\nStill, all these little eggs contained an extra treat with their toys. They  __35__  notes from afar.\nThere was just one problem for the German children who received them: They were written in Russian.", "matchIntro": "Hyphenating your last name after marriage?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Congratulations on deciding to get married! While being married is great and wonderful, the act of getting married can be quite stressful. There is the planning of the ceremony itself, the merging(合并) of two households and all of the details involved in that. You also have to take the time to apply for your marriage license and then decide whether or not you are going to keep your last name or change it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "This is where a lot of people have trouble. Once upon a time, it was just customary for a bride to take her groom's last name as her own and abandon her maiden name completely. The act of keeping her own last name was considered taboo(禁忌) and people's eyebrows would raise right off their faces when they found out that the bride was even considering something so radical. Over time, though, more and more women are deciding to keep their last names."}, {"label": "C", "text": "There are a lot of reasons to want to keep your own last name. Hopefully you have a supportive groom who understands why this idea is the most appealing to you. A lot of times, though, the act of keeping your name is still something that causes concern. Even if your groom is fine with it, his family (or your own) might not be so understanding."}, {"label": "D", "text": "There are a couple of ways to compromise on the issue. You could choose an entirely new last name for the two of you to share. This way neither of you gets \"your\" way and you both have to deal with the legalities of going through a name change. Most of the time, though, the most popular compromise is to hyphenate your last name and the last name of your groom. For example, if your groom's name is John Smith and your name is Kate Jones, you would name yourself Kate Jones-Smith or Kate Smith-Jones. This allows you to keep going by your own last name while legally adopting your husband's last name at the same time."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The compromise is the biggest reason that so many women choose to hyphenate their last names. It is a way of you keeping your own identity while also making your future husband happy. It allows you to stay connected to accomplishments that you achieved before you got married. For example, many women who choose to hyphenate do so partially because they have earned higher educational degrees and certifications under their maiden names. They might have also had things published or publicized and want to stay connected to that identity. Hyphenating your last name can help you bridge the gap between your personal and professional lives. Many women who opt to simply keep their own names do so because they don't want to give up all that they have accomplished professionally. Adopting their husband's last name makes it hard for them to claim ownership of these accomplishments personally because they happened under their \"other\" name."}, {"label": "F", "text": "While tradition is one thing, there isn't any logical reason to completely change your name. Keeping your name and joining it to your husband's through hyphenation is as legal as simply adopting his name or as simply keeping your own and leaving his out of the picture entirely."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Probably the most important reason to consider hyphenation is your identity. You've spent your whole life building your identity under a certain name. Obviously you will still be you even if you've taken on your husband's last name and omitted yours entirely. At the same time, your name is associated with the identity you've built up and hyphenation allows you to respect that while also respecting tradition and your husband's family's identity."}, {"label": "H", "text": "If you care about outside opinions on your name, you should know that a large portion of today's society is annoyed by the hyphenated name. Some people find it \"snobby\"(势利的) and others simply find it irritating because they have a hard time remembering which last name they are supposed to say first. Some people even believe that not simply adopting your husband's last name is a huge sign of disrespect and a lack of commitment."}, {"label": "I", "text": "It is possible that your future husband will find this choice offensive. Some men, regardless of how you might feel about such things, are traditionalists and feel that it is simply \"right\" for the woman to take the man's last name as her own. Whether your future husband insisting on your adopting his last name is a red flag to you or not, it is still something that you should take into consideration."}, {"label": "J", "text": "There are all sorts of reasons to hyphenate your name or not to hyphenate your name. The basis for each of these things, however, is whether or not you are willing to make a compromise when it comes to changing your name. The hyphenation is the epitome(体现) of a compromise. One spouse wants a complete name change. The other spouse wants no name change. Hyphenating the two names is a way for each person to, at least a little bit, \"win\" the argument."}, {"label": "K", "text": "And don't forget the legal stuff! Making the decision is just part of the process. From there you are going to have to legally change your name all over the place. You'll need to change your name at the bank, with social security, with your credit cards, on your driver's license, at the gym…everywhere. It's easy to get overwhelmed just thinking about it."}, {"label": "L", "text": "What matters, of course, is that the two of you are going to be happily married—hopefully for the rest of your lives. At the end of the day, whether you each keep your names, whether you come up with an entirely new name for the two of you to share or whether you hyphenate your current last names, what matters is that you love each other and are going to be joining your lives together. Try to remember that as you are staring at the application for your marriage license and filling out the portion that reads \"name after you get married\" (or whatever the legalese for that might be)."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "It's good to be smart. After all, intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and even live longer. But there's another side to the story. The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense.\nTravis Bradberry, president at TalentSmart, explained in his post Why Smart People Act So Stupid that \"Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand. Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they use logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their reasoning abilities.\" They are so used to being right and having quick answers that they don't even realise when they're making a mess by answering without thinking things through.\nA lifetime of praise leads smart people to develop too much faith in their intelligence and abilities. They often fail to recognise when they need help, and when they do recognise it, they tend to believe that no one else is capable of providing it.\n\"It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong. It's even harder for smart people because they grow so used to being right all the time that it becomes a part of their identity,\" Bradberry wrote. \"For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\"\nSmart people also have a hard time accepting feedback. They tend to undervalue the opinions of others, which means they have trouble believing that anyone is qualified to give them useful feedback. Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful relationships, both personally and professionally.\nSmart people develop overachieving personalities because things come so easily to them. They simply don't understand how hard some people have to work to accomplish the same things, and because of that, they push people too hard. They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they assume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.", "47": "It's good to be smart. After all, intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and even live longer. But there's another side to the story. The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense.\nTravis Bradberry, president at TalentSmart, explained in his post Why Smart People Act So Stupid that \"Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand. Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they use logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their reasoning abilities.\" They are so used to being right and having quick answers that they don't even realise when they're making a mess by answering without thinking things through.\nA lifetime of praise leads smart people to develop too much faith in their intelligence and abilities. They often fail to recognise when they need help, and when they do recognise it, they tend to believe that no one else is capable of providing it.\n\"It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong. It's even harder for smart people because they grow so used to being right all the time that it becomes a part of their identity,\" Bradberry wrote. \"For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\"\nSmart people also have a hard time accepting feedback. They tend to undervalue the opinions of others, which means they have trouble believing that anyone is qualified to give them useful feedback. Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful relationships, both personally and professionally.\nSmart people develop overachieving personalities because things come so easily to them. They simply don't understand how hard some people have to work to accomplish the same things, and because of that, they push people too hard. They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they assume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.", "48": "It's good to be smart. After all, intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and even live longer. But there's another side to the story. The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense.\nTravis Bradberry, president at TalentSmart, explained in his post Why Smart People Act So Stupid that \"Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand. Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they use logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their reasoning abilities.\" They are so used to being right and having quick answers that they don't even realise when they're making a mess by answering without thinking things through.\nA lifetime of praise leads smart people to develop too much faith in their intelligence and abilities. They often fail to recognise when they need help, and when they do recognise it, they tend to believe that no one else is capable of providing it.\n\"It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong. It's even harder for smart people because they grow so used to being right all the time that it becomes a part of their identity,\" Bradberry wrote. \"For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\"\nSmart people also have a hard time accepting feedback. They tend to undervalue the opinions of others, which means they have trouble believing that anyone is qualified to give them useful feedback. Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful relationships, both personally and professionally.\nSmart people develop overachieving personalities because things come so easily to them. They simply don't understand how hard some people have to work to accomplish the same things, and because of that, they push people too hard. They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they assume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.", "49": "It's good to be smart. After all, intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and even live longer. But there's another side to the story. The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense.\nTravis Bradberry, president at TalentSmart, explained in his post Why Smart People Act So Stupid that \"Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand. Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they use logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their reasoning abilities.\" They are so used to being right and having quick answers that they don't even realise when they're making a mess by answering without thinking things through.\nA lifetime of praise leads smart people to develop too much faith in their intelligence and abilities. They often fail to recognise when they need help, and when they do recognise it, they tend to believe that no one else is capable of providing it.\n\"It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong. It's even harder for smart people because they grow so used to being right all the time that it becomes a part of their identity,\" Bradberry wrote. \"For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\"\nSmart people also have a hard time accepting feedback. They tend to undervalue the opinions of others, which means they have trouble believing that anyone is qualified to give them useful feedback. Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful relationships, both personally and professionally.\nSmart people develop overachieving personalities because things come so easily to them. They simply don't understand how hard some people have to work to accomplish the same things, and because of that, they push people too hard. They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they assume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.", "50": "It's good to be smart. After all, intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and even live longer. But there's another side to the story. The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense.\nTravis Bradberry, president at TalentSmart, explained in his post Why Smart People Act So Stupid that \"Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand. Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they use logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their reasoning abilities.\" They are so used to being right and having quick answers that they don't even realise when they're making a mess by answering without thinking things through.\nA lifetime of praise leads smart people to develop too much faith in their intelligence and abilities. They often fail to recognise when they need help, and when they do recognise it, they tend to believe that no one else is capable of providing it.\n\"It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong. It's even harder for smart people because they grow so used to being right all the time that it becomes a part of their identity,\" Bradberry wrote. \"For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\"\nSmart people also have a hard time accepting feedback. They tend to undervalue the opinions of others, which means they have trouble believing that anyone is qualified to give them useful feedback. Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful relationships, both personally and professionally.\nSmart people develop overachieving personalities because things come so easily to them. They simply don't understand how hard some people have to work to accomplish the same things, and because of that, they push people too hard. They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they assume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.", "51": "Of the endless troubles that come with being driven from one's home country, losing educational certificates may seem small. But it isn't. Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their previous career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\nThe State Department will announce a partnership with an online education platform called Coursera. The platform will allow refugees worldwide to take thousands of online courses for free. \"Coursera for Refugees\" will be available for any non-profit group that supports refugees in any country, as well as individual refugees. They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can take all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform currently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to fashion design.\nAs the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern. The new program aims to aid refugees by offering \"important skills that will help them in the global economy,\" Evan Ryan, U.S. assistant secretary of state for educational and cultural affairs, said on a press call last week. \"What we don't want to do is not act,\" added Coursera chief operations officer Lila Ibrahim.\nThe idea isn't without problems. For one, starting a new career path isn't as simple as watching a few videos and obtaining an online certificate; even those looking to continue previous careers can't simply rely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and practices. For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add translations, language barriers can be a big obstacle. Then there are the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs) themselves—both for refugees and for average learners.\nBut it's still early days and there are signs of hope. In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career benefits afterwards.", "52": "Of the endless troubles that come with being driven from one's home country, losing educational certificates may seem small. But it isn't. Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their previous career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\nThe State Department will announce a partnership with an online education platform called Coursera. The platform will allow refugees worldwide to take thousands of online courses for free. \"Coursera for Refugees\" will be available for any non-profit group that supports refugees in any country, as well as individual refugees. They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can take all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform currently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to fashion design.\nAs the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern. The new program aims to aid refugees by offering \"important skills that will help them in the global economy,\" Evan Ryan, U.S. assistant secretary of state for educational and cultural affairs, said on a press call last week. \"What we don't want to do is not act,\" added Coursera chief operations officer Lila Ibrahim.\nThe idea isn't without problems. For one, starting a new career path isn't as simple as watching a few videos and obtaining an online certificate; even those looking to continue previous careers can't simply rely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and practices. For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add translations, language barriers can be a big obstacle. Then there are the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs) themselves—both for refugees and for average learners.\nBut it's still early days and there are signs of hope. In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career benefits afterwards.", "53": "Of the endless troubles that come with being driven from one's home country, losing educational certificates may seem small. But it isn't. Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their previous career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\nThe State Department will announce a partnership with an online education platform called Coursera. The platform will allow refugees worldwide to take thousands of online courses for free. \"Coursera for Refugees\" will be available for any non-profit group that supports refugees in any country, as well as individual refugees. They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can take all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform currently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to fashion design.\nAs the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern. The new program aims to aid refugees by offering \"important skills that will help them in the global economy,\" Evan Ryan, U.S. assistant secretary of state for educational and cultural affairs, said on a press call last week. \"What we don't want to do is not act,\" added Coursera chief operations officer Lila Ibrahim.\nThe idea isn't without problems. For one, starting a new career path isn't as simple as watching a few videos and obtaining an online certificate; even those looking to continue previous careers can't simply rely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and practices. For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add translations, language barriers can be a big obstacle. Then there are the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs) themselves—both for refugees and for average learners.\nBut it's still early days and there are signs of hope. In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career benefits afterwards.", "54": "Of the endless troubles that come with being driven from one's home country, losing educational certificates may seem small. But it isn't. Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their previous career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\nThe State Department will announce a partnership with an online education platform called Coursera. The platform will allow refugees worldwide to take thousands of online courses for free. \"Coursera for Refugees\" will be available for any non-profit group that supports refugees in any country, as well as individual refugees. They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can take all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform currently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to fashion design.\nAs the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern. The new program aims to aid refugees by offering \"important skills that will help them in the global economy,\" Evan Ryan, U.S. assistant secretary of state for educational and cultural affairs, said on a press call last week. \"What we don't want to do is not act,\" added Coursera chief operations officer Lila Ibrahim.\nThe idea isn't without problems. For one, starting a new career path isn't as simple as watching a few videos and obtaining an online certificate; even those looking to continue previous careers can't simply rely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and practices. For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add translations, language barriers can be a big obstacle. Then there are the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs) themselves—both for refugees and for average learners.\nBut it's still early days and there are signs of hope. In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career benefits afterwards.", "55": "Of the endless troubles that come with being driven from one's home country, losing educational certificates may seem small. But it isn't. Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their previous career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\nThe State Department will announce a partnership with an online education platform called Coursera. The platform will allow refugees worldwide to take thousands of online courses for free. \"Coursera for Refugees\" will be available for any non-profit group that supports refugees in any country, as well as individual refugees. They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can take all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform currently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to fashion design.\nAs the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern. The new program aims to aid refugees by offering \"important skills that will help them in the global economy,\" Evan Ryan, U.S. assistant secretary of state for educational and cultural affairs, said on a press call last week. \"What we don't want to do is not act,\" added Coursera chief operations officer Lila Ibrahim.\nThe idea isn't without problems. For one, starting a new career path isn't as simple as watching a few videos and obtaining an online certificate; even those looking to continue previous careers can't simply rely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and practices. For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add translations, language barriers can be a big obstacle. Then there are the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs) themselves—both for refugees and for average learners.\nBut it's still early days and there are signs of hope. In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career benefits afterwards."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-03-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the news report mainly about?", "options": ["A) A proposed policy allowing Africans to travel in Africa without a visa.", "B) An agreement among 13 African countries to set up a free-trade zone.", "C) A plan to invite all African countries to join the African Union.", "D) An important initiative to permit tourists to visit Africa without a visa."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） A proposed policy allowing Africans to travel in Africa without a visa.\n【定位】African leaders plan to turn the continent into a continent without borders. They will\nintroduce a single passport policy, allowing free movement between countries.\n非洲各国领导人计划把整个非洲大陆变成一个没有边界的大陆。他们将推出统一护照政策，\n允许各国之间自由通行。\n【信号】开头点题即主旨。新闻类听力的导语句几乎就是主旨句。African leaders plan to turn the\ncontinent into a continent without borders. 这一句开门见山讲\"非洲领导人要让整个非\n洲免边界（即免签）\"，紧接着 They will introduce a single passport policy, allowing\nfree movement between countries. 直接落到具体政策上——\"政策+对象\"全在这里。\n【替换】turn the continent into a continent without borders ↔ travel in Africa without a visa\n\"无边界\"=\"免签通行\"\nallowing free movement between countries ↔ Africans to travel in Africa\nfree movement=travel\nThe African Union, which represents 54 states, wants to abolish the requirement of a visa for\nAfricans visiting other African countries.\n↔ A proposed policy allowing Africans to travel in Africa without a visa.\n原文反复点 Africans+visa\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了录音 Currently, 13 African countries have visa-free deals in place with each\nother. 里的 13 African countries 这个数字原词，但原文说的是\"目前已有 13 国互相免\n签\"，根本不是\"13 国设立自贸区\"——自贸区在原文里是另一桩\"未来要做的事\"，不限于 13\n国。\n借用原文：Currently, 13 African countries have visa-free deals in place with each other.\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 盗用了原文 The African Union, which represents 54 states（非盟代表 54 个国家）中\n的 African Union 这一专名，制造\"听到非盟就想选\"的诱惑；但原文谈的是非盟\"代表\"54 国\n并推动免签自贸，根本没说要\"邀请各国加入非盟\"——加入与否不是本新闻的话题。\n借用原文：The African Union, which represents 54 states\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 用 tourists 替换了原文里的 Africans——原文 Americans are able to travel to 20 of\nthe continent's countries without a visa 是在拿\"美国人（外来游客的代表）现状\"与\"非洲\n人现状\"作对照，目的恰恰是说\"非洲人反而不如外人方便、所以才要改\"。把主体从\"非洲\n人\"偷换到\"游客\"，正好与新闻核心相反。\n借用原文：Americans are able to travel to 20 of the continent's countries without a visa", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What benefit will a free trade deal bring to African economy?", "options": ["A) It will attract more investments from all over the world.", "B) It will help many African countries reduce trade deficits.", "C) It will reduce the cost of trade between African countries.", "D) It will allow Africans to play a bigger role in world trade."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It will reduce the cost of trade between African countries.\n【定位】It also wants to establish a free trade deal across the continent in the near future, as trade\nwithin the African continent costs more than any other region.\n（非盟）还希望在不久的将来在整个非洲大陆建立自由贸易协定，因为目前非洲大陆内部的\n贸易成本比其他任何地区都要高。\n【信号】as 引出原因即落点。答案句里出现因果信号词 as trade within the African continent\ncosts more than any other region——as 在这里是\"因为\"。\"自贸要解决的问题=非洲内\n部贸易最贵\"，反过来推就是\"自贸的好处=降低非洲内部贸易成本\"。这就是听力\"信号词后\n是落点\"的典型用法。\n【替换】trade within the African continent costs more than any other region ↔\nreduce the cost of trade between African countries\n因果对调＋反义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇没提到 investment / invest 任何形式的词。A 听起来\"很正确\"是因为它符合考生\n对\"自贸好处\"的常识联想，但题目问的是录音里给出的好处，不是考生想到的好处。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 用 trade deficits（贸易逆差）替换了原文真正说的 trade costs（贸易成本）。\"贸易成\n本\"和\"贸易逆差\"是两个完全不同的经济概念——前者是\"做生意要花多少钱\"，后者是\"进口\n比出口多多少\"。选项利用了 trade 这个原词来诱选。\n借用原文：trade within the African continent costs more than any other region\nD) 过度推断\nD 说的 a bigger role in world trade（在世界贸易中扮演更大角色）把范围从\"非洲内部\"扩\n到了\"全世界\"。原文里 free trade deal 限定的范围是 across the continent（在整个非洲\n大陆），目标是降低非洲国家之间的贸易成本，并未谈到非洲在世界贸易里的地位变化。\n借用原文：establish a free trade deal across the continent\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Researchers in Iceland found a new way of tackling climate change by pumping carbon dioxide\nunderground and turning it into stone. Other carbon capture and storage methods store carbon dioxide as\na gas. But problems include a high cost and concern about leaks. This new method of burying carbon\ndioxide and turning it into stone is cheaper and more secure, the Guardian reports. To turn carbon\ndioxide into stone, researchers with the CarbFix project pumped the gas into volcanic rock and sped up\nthe natural process, in which the volcanic rock reacts with gas and forms carbon minerals. The gas\nturned into solid in just two years, much faster than the hundreds or thousands of years researchers had\npredicted. The research took place at Iceland's Hellisheidi power plant. Already, the project in Iceland\nhas been increased in scale to bury 10,000 tons of carbon dioxide each year. One potential difficulty is\nthat for each ton of carbon dioxide buried, the technique requires 25 tons of water. However, the lead\nresearcher said sea water could be used.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What new method has been developed to help fight climate change?", "options": ["A) Cooling down volcanic rock with sea water.", "B) Storing carbon dioxide underground as a gas.", "C) Capturing carbon dioxide and burying it under the sea.", "D) Pumping carbon dioxide underground to form stone."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Pumping carbon dioxide underground to form stone.\n【定位】Researchers in Iceland found a new way of tackling climate change by pumping carbon\ndioxide underground and turning it into stone.\n冰岛的研究人员发现了一种应对气候变化的新方法：把二氧化碳泵入地下，将其变成石头。\n【信号】首句点题信号 a new way。Researchers in Iceland found a new way of tackling\nclimate change 中的 found a new way 与题干 new method 形成同义对接，是新闻典型\n的开门见山首句点题，这一句直接承载新方法是什么的答案。\n【替换】pumping carbon dioxide underground ↔ Pumping carbon dioxide underground\n原词同形复现\nturning it into stone ↔ to form stone\nturn into = form 的动词同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项把录音里两处出现的 volcanic rock 与 sea water 拼成 cooling down volcanic rock\nwith sea water，但录音从未说过冷却火山岩这件事，cooling 是凭空造的动作。\n借用原文：pumped the gas into volcanic rock\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音原文 Other carbon capture and storage methods store carbon dioxide as a gas.\n明确说把二氧化碳以气体形式储存是其他/已有方法，本则新闻要讲的恰恰是与之对照的新\n方法；选项 B 把旧方法当成新方法的答案，是典型张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Other carbon capture and storage methods store carbon dioxide as a gas.\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说的是 burying carbon dioxide and turning it into stone，埋的地方是地下\n（underground / volcanic rock），但选项 C 写成 burying it under the sea，把地点偷\n换成海底；只是单词长得像，意思完全偏。\n借用原文：burying carbon dioxide and turning it into stone", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What is a potential difficulty in applying the new technique?", "options": ["A) Lack of burying ground.", "B) Long time for processing.", "C) High consumption of water.", "D) Enormous cost of energy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） High consumption of water.\n【定位】One potential difficulty is that for each ton of carbon dioxide buried, the technique requires\n25 tons of water.\n一个潜在的困难是，每埋藏一吨二氧化碳，这项技术需要 25 吨水。\n【信号】题干原词信号 One potential difficulty。One potential difficulty is that 与题干\npotential difficulty 用词几乎完全一致，是录音替我们点出的答案句信号词；这是新闻收尾\n段最常见的转折/补充结构，信号词后那一句就是承载答案的句子。\n【替换】requires 25 tons of water ↔ High consumption of water\n数量级 → 程度词，动词 → 名词化\nfor each ton of carbon dioxide buried, the technique requires 25 tons of water ↔\nHigh consumption of water\n1∶25 比例支撑 High 这一判断\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音并未提到埋藏地点不够这件事，反而提到 the project in Iceland has been increased\nin scale to bury 10,000 tons of carbon dioxide each year，说明埋藏规模在扩大，不是\n缺地的问题；选项 A 属于无中生有。\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说的是 The gas turned into solid in just two years, much faster than the\nhundreds or thousands of years researchers had predicted.，明确强调这项技术比预期\n快得多，并不是耗时长；选项 B 取了 hundreds of years 这一原词却反了方向。\n借用原文：The gas turned into solid in just two years, much faster than the hundreds or thousands\nof years researchers had predicted.\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里的 Other carbon capture and storage methods store carbon dioxide as a gas.\nBut problems include a high cost and concern about leaks. 中的 high cost 是描述其\n他/旧方法的问题，且讲的是费用而非 energy；选项 D 把 cost 与 energy 拼到一起，既张\n冠李戴（说的是旧方法）、又偷换概念（cost ≠ energy）。\n借用原文：problems include a high cost and concern about leaks", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the news report say about French people's bread consumption?", "options": ["A) It varies with the seasons.", "B) It has been on the decline.", "C) It has caused nationwide concern.", "D) It is ninety grams daily on average."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It has been on the decline.\n【定位】The National Association of French Millers says bread consumption is on the decline, with\nFrench people eating nine grams less than in the previous year.\n法国全国磨坊主协会表示，面包消费正在下滑，法国人比前一年少吃了 9 克。\n【信号】权威主体+趋势动词直陈。The National Association of French Millers says bread\nconsumption is on the decline 一句里，权威主体 says 引出对消费状况的直接断言，is\non the decline 是清晰的趋势判定信号，是本题事实陈述题的典型答案句标志。\n【替换】bread consumption is on the decline ↔ It has been on the decline.\n几乎原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt varies with the seasons. 在录音里找不到任何 season/seasonal/winter/summer 之类\n的踪迹，属于完全凭空捏造的方向。\nC)\n录音里说 The association is concerned about the effects this trend will have on their\ntrade，关注者是行业协会、关注对象是他们的生意，选项 nationwide concern 把范围扩\n大到了'全国关注'，是典型的范围/程度放大。\n借用原文：The association is concerned about the effects this trend will have on their trade\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里的 90 grams 出现在 women eat 80 to 90 grams，特指女性消费区间的上限，不是\n全体法国人的日均量；选项 It is ninety grams daily on average. 盗用了原文中的 90\ngrams 这个原词，却安到了'整体平均'头上，是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：women eat 80 to 90 grams", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn about French women from their port?", "options": ["A) They emphasize food variety.", "B) They prefer French stick loaves.", "C) They do more cooking than men.", "D) They favor diets lower in calories."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They favor diets lower in calories.\n【定位】Firstly, the association cited changing diets, in particular, the switch to those low in calories,\nas a factor in the reduction of bread consumption. This could explain why the difference in\nconsumption between genders is so big. Nowadays, men eat an approximate average of 140\ngrams of bread daily, whereas women eat 80 to 90 grams.\n首先，协会指出饮食结构的变化、特别是转向低卡饮食，是面包消费下降的一个原因。这或\n许能解释为什么男女之间的消费差距如此之大。如今男性日均吃约 140 克面包，而女性只吃\n80 到 90 克。\n【信号】因果链 explain why+男女对比。the switch to those low in calories 与 This could\nexplain why the difference in consumption between genders is so big 通过 explain\nwhy 把低卡饮食与男女消费差异连成因果链，男性 140g/女性 80–90g 的对比正好印证女\n性更倾向低卡，这条因果链就是本题的答案信号。\n【替换】the switch to those low in calories ↔ favor diets lower in calories\nthe switch to=favor 的同义改写\nwomen eat 80 to 90 grams ↔ favor diets lower in calories\n对比数据印证偏好\n【排除】A)\nThey emphasize food variety. 在录音里找不到 variety/different kinds 之类的对应表达，\n属于无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音 People in France are keeping away from the narrow French stick loaf 明确说法国\n人是'远离'法棍，而 They prefer French stick loaves. 偷换为'偏爱'，方向正好相反，且盗\n用了 French stick loaves 这一原词制造熟悉感。\n借用原文：People in France are keeping away from the narrow French stick loaf\nC) 偷换概念\nThey do more cooking than men. 在录音里完全没有 cook/cooking 的对应词，且男女\n对比的对象始终是'吃多少面包'而非'谁下厨'，属于编造对比维度。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is said about lifestyle changes of the French people?", "options": ["A) They bake more bread at home.", "B) They spend less time eating breakfast.", "C) They eat more fruit than they used to.", "D) They put jam instead of butter on bread."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They spend less time eating breakfast.\n【定位】The second reason they say is lifestyle changes in the country, with people dedicating less\ntime to breakfast when bread would traditionally be eaten with butter and jam.\n他们说的第二个原因是这个国家的生活方式发生了变化——人们花在早餐上的时间变少了，\n而早餐传统上是把面包配上黄油和果酱来吃的。\n【信号】明示 lifestyle changes 标题句。The second reason they say is lifestyle changes in\nthe country 直接以 lifestyle changes 标题切入，紧接的 dedicating less time to\nbreakfast 就是这条生活方式变化的具体内容，是本题答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】dedicating less time to breakfast ↔ They spend less time eating breakfast.\ndedicate=spend 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey bake more bread at home. 在录音里找不到 bake/at home 的踪迹，是无中生有的\n方向。\nC)\neat more fruit than they used to 用了 used to 这种生活方式变化的常见框架来伪装，但\nfruit 在录音里完全没出现，属于借框架编内容。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 bread would traditionally be eaten with butter and jam 表明的是传统上面包\n是'同时'配黄油和果酱来吃，并不存在'用果酱取代黄油'。选项 put jam instead of butter\non bread 盗用了 butter 和 jam 两个原词，又添加 instead of 制造一个原文没有的替换关\n系。\n借用原文：bread would traditionally be eaten with butter and jam", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "When would the man like to leave for London?", "options": ["A) After the rush hour.", "B) Early in the morning.", "C) As soon as possible.", "D) Around lunch time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） After the rush hour.\n【定位】Well, after the rush hour, I think. We'd like to avoid the crowds if possible. So, probably\nsometime around 10 o'clock.\n嗯，我想是早高峰之后吧。我们希望尽量避开人潮。所以大概十点钟左右。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。前一句售票员问 What time would you like to go?，紧接着男士开口\nWell, after the rush hour, I think.——这是\"问—答\"结构里答的那一句，是听力定位答案\n最稳的信号。\n【替换】after the rush hour ↔ After the rush hour.\n原句原词整体回收\navoid the crowds ↔ After the rush hour.\n因果同义印证\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音里没有任何 early in the morning 的表述；恰恰相反，男士说 after the rush hour，\n明显是\"避开早高峰\"——选 B 等于把\"早高峰之后\"反着理解成\"清早\"。\nC) 与原文相反\nAs soon as possible. 与男士 after the rush hour avoid the crowds 的意思相反——他要\n的是错开高峰、不是越早越好。\n借用原文：after the rush hour\nD) 过度推断\n男士说 sometime around 10 o'clock，10 点不是午餐时间；选 D 是把\"10 点\"误推到\nlunch time，属于对原词的过度引申。\n借用原文：around 10 o'clock", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the man's other request?", "options": ["A) Two first class seats.", "B) Two seats together.", "C) One window seat.", "D) One seat near the exit."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） One window seat.\n【定位】Second class is fine. Thanks. Is there any chance we could have a window seat for one\nof those?\n二等座就行，谢谢。其中一张能不能给我们安排一个靠窗的座位？\n【信号】请求句 Is there any chance。男士在确认席别后用 Is there any chance we could have\na window seat for one of those? 主动提出一个\"额外要求\"，这种请求句式是答案信号；\n前半 Second class is fine. 是先答售票员问题，真正的 other request 在 chance 之后。\n【替换】a window seat for one of those ↔ One window seat.\n数量+席位类型逐项对齐\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n男士明确说 Second class is fine.——选的是二等座，不是头等座；A 项盗用了对话里讨论\n过的 first class 话题词，但极性反了。\n借用原文：Second class is fine.\nB) 张冠李戴\nI've booked those seats next to each other for you. 是售票员主动给出的服务，不是男\n士提出的请求；B 项盗用了 next to each other = together 的语义，但来源不是男士——\n属于把售票员的话安到男士头上。\n借用原文：I've booked those seats next to each other for you.\nD) 未提及\nOne seat near the exit. 里的 exit 在录音中完全没念，属于凭常识或对窗口座位的反义臆\n造出的干扰项。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Where should the man wait for the train?", "options": ["A) In the middle of the platform.", "B) At the far end of the platform.", "C) Behind the yellow line.", "D) In the waiting room."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） At the far end of the platform.\n【定位】All you need to do is wait at the far end of the platform.\n你们要做的就是在站台的最远端等候。\n【信号】All you need to do 的指示句。All you need to do is wait at the far end of the\nplatform. 用 All you need to do 这种强调式的祈使指示句，等于给出一条明确指令，是答\n案信号；后面紧跟 When the train arrives, tell the train guard... 是补充流程，不是该等的\n地点。\n【替换】at the far end of the platform ↔ At the far end of the platform.\n原句完整搬运\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音只说在 the far end of the platform 等车，没有说 middle；A 项盗用了原文里的\nplatform 一词，把方位偷换成 middle。\n借用原文：at the far end of the platform\nC) 未提及\n录音里完全没提 yellow line；这是日常乘车经验里的安全规则，主体考生容易凭母语常识硬\n选。\nD) 未提及\n录音里也没提 waiting room；男士被告知直接到 the far end of the platform 等车，不是\n去候车室。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What should the man do to collect the bicycles?", "options": ["A) Wait in a queue for his turn.", "B) Go to the baggage claim area.", "C) Pay an extra fee for the service.", "D) Give the ticket to the train guard."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Give the ticket to the train guard.\n【定位】When you arrive in London, hand the ticket to the guard and he will return the bicycles\nto you.\n等你们到了伦敦，把票交给乘务员，他会把自行车还给你们。\n【信号】When you arrive 的时间状语+祈使。When you arrive in London, hand the ticket to\nthe guard and he will return the bicycles to you. 这一句的 When you arrive in\nLondon 正好对应题干 to collect the bicycles 的\"到达后\"语境；hand the ticket 是祈使动\n词，他/还给你 是结果——这是地道的答案信号。\n【替换】hand the ticket to the guard ↔ Give the ticket to the train guard.\n同义动词替换\nhe will return the bicycles to you ↔ Give the ticket to the train guard.\n因果配套印证\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里没有 wait in a queue 或类似的排队动作；A 项是凭常识\"想象车站要排队\"硬塞的。\nB) 偷换概念\nbaggage claim area 是机场用语；录音里说的是 he will help you secure the bicycles in\nthe last carriage——在火车最后一节车厢交接，不存在行李提取区。B 项盗用了与\"取\n回\"语义相似的航空场景词。\n借用原文：he will help you secure the bicycles in the last carriage\nC) 与原文相反\n售票员明确说 It's no extra cost. The price will be the same.——不会再收额外费用；C\n项却说要 pay an extra fee，与原文极性正相反。\n借用原文：It's no extra cost.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about hat factories in Britain?", "options": ["A) Almost all of them were operating at a deficit.", "B) They have all been turned into movie theatres.", "C) Nearly all of them closed down decades ago.", "D) They have adapted to meet fashion changes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Nearly all of them closed down decades ago.\n【定位】There used to be many hat factories in Britain until around the 1950s, but fashion changed\nand most people stopped wearing hats. So practically all of those factories had to close down.\n英国曾经有很多帽厂，一直延续到 1950 年代左右；但后来潮流变了，大多数人不再戴帽\n子，所以那些工厂几乎全都倒闭了。\n【信号】but + So 的双重连接信号。but fashion changed 的 but 把话题从「曾经繁荣」掰向「后\n来衰落」，紧接 So practically all of those factories had to close down. 的 So 又把因果\n一锤定音——「but 转折 + So 结论」这一组连贯信号，正是答案句的标志。\n【替换】practically all of those factories had to close down ↔\nNearly all of them closed down decades ago.\npractically ≈ nearly，1950s = decades ago\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从头到尾没提帽厂亏损的事，Almost all of them were operating at a deficit. 是把\n「衰落」这个事实换成了「亏损」这个具体的财务原因，原文并未给出。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey have all been turned into movie theatres. 盗用了 we transformed it into a\nmovie theater 中「改造成电影院」的措辞——但录音明说这件事只发生在「这一家」帽厂\n（This one, in particular, was closed for 47 years before we transformed it into a\nmovie theater.），把单数案例扩大到全部，是典型张冠李戴。\n借用原文：This one, in particular, was closed for 47 years before we transformed it into a\nmovie theater.\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说 fashion changed and most people stopped wearing hats 才导致工厂关门——\n是被潮流「抛弃」，而非 They have adapted to meet fashion changes. 所说的「成功顺\n应潮流」，极性正好相反。\n借用原文：fashion changed and most people stopped wearing hats", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What is special about Cube Movie Theater?", "options": ["A) It shows foreign movies exclusively.", "B) It shows movies from the 1950s.", "C) It is the oldest theatre in London.", "D) It is located next to a hat factory."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It shows foreign movies exclusively.\n【定位】We are the only movie theater in the whole country that only shows foreign movies.\n我们是全国唯一一家只放外语电影的影院。\n【信号】the only + only 双重独家信号。the only movie theater in the whole country 用 the\nonly 给独特性钉了第一颗钉子，that only shows foreign movies 又用 only 把范围进一步\n缩死——「the only ... only」这种双重限定，是答案句最强的信号。\n【替换】only shows foreign movies ↔ It shows foreign movies exclusively.\nonly ↔ exclusively 的同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIt shows movies from the 1950s. 把第 12 题里出现过的 until around the 1950s 这个时\n间数字盗过来当影院题材，制造「听到 1950s 就想选」的诱惑——可 1950s 在录音里讲的\n是「帽厂的年代」，而非影院上映的电影年代，是典型的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：until around the 1950s\nC) 未提及\nIt is the oldest theatre in London. 录音里压根没提「最老」，反而说 Cube Movie\nTheater has been around now for 11 years.——只有 11 年的影院显然算不上 oldest。\n借用原文：Cube Movie Theater has been around now for 11 years.\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt is located next to a hat factory. 利用了录音里 It's located in what used to be a hat\nfactory. 的措辞——但原文说的是「影院开在原先的帽厂里」，next to（紧邻）与 in what\nused to be（开在旧址里）位置关系完全不同，是典型的偷换位置概念。\n借用原文：It's located in what used to be a hat factory.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say about most people in London?", "options": ["A) They wear hats on social occasions.", "B) They don't speak foreign languages.", "C) They enjoy watching foreign movies.", "D) They don't go to movie theatres often."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They don't speak foreign languages.\n【定位】Is there enough demand for that? I would have thought that in London, most people don't\nspeak foreign languages.\n这种电影真有那么多人看吗？我本以为伦敦的大多数人是不懂外语的。\n【信号】I would have thought + most people don't 的观点信号。I would have thought that\n... 是男方亮出自己看法的标志性结构，紧跟其后的 most people don't speak foreign\nlanguages 就是他对伦敦多数人的判断——「I thought / I would have thought」之后那\n句是观点句，必抓。\n【替换】most people don't speak foreign languages ↔ They don't speak foreign languages.\n几乎原句对原句，主语等值替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey wear hats on social occasions. 利用录音里反复出现的 most people stopped\nwearing hats 这个词组制造熟悉感——但原文是说英国人「不再戴帽」，并非「在社交场\n合戴帽」，且这句话是关于「英国人对帽子的态度」，不是「伦敦人对外语的态度」，话题\n与说话人都对不上。\n借用原文：most people stopped wearing hats\nC) 与原文相反\nThey enjoy watching foreign movies. 的极性正好和男方观点相反——男方明说 most\npeople don't speak foreign languages，正是在质疑「demand 够不够」，而非肯定他们\n爱看外语片；而后女方才反驳说外语片仍有市场，但那是女方的话，不是男方的。\n借用原文：most people don't speak foreign languages\nD) 未提及\nThey don't go to movie theatres often. 是关于伦敦人「去影院频率」的描述，录音里从\n未提及伦敦人「常不常去影院」，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman say about foreign movies shown in London?", "options": ["A) They are meant mostly for immigrants.", "B) They attract large crowds of young Londoners.", "C) They are hard for English people to appreciate.", "D) They have an English translation on the screen."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have an English translation on the screen.\n【定位】Furthermore, I might add, all movies come with English text at the bottom of the screen. So\neven if you don't understand what the actors say, you can still read it.\n另外我得补一句，所有的电影都在屏幕下方配有英文文字，所以哪怕听不懂演员说什么，你\n也能读字幕。\n【信号】Furthermore + I might add 的补充强调信号。女方一连用 Furthermore 和 I might add\n两个补充强调标志把这条信息推到台前，紧跟其后给出「屏幕下方有英文」这一具体事实\n——「Furthermore + I might add」组合是答主特别想让人记住的那一句的信号灯。\n【替换】all movies come with English text at the bottom of the screen ↔\nThey have an English translation on the screen.\nEnglish text ↔ English translation；at the bottom of the screen ↔ on the screen\neven if you don't understand what the actors say, you can still read it ↔\nThey have an English translation on the screen.\n补充句佐证字幕功能\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are meant mostly for immigrants. 把外语片的受众限定为「移民」，录音里完全没\n出现 immigrants 这个话题，是凭常识脑补出来的无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey attract large crowds of young Londoners. 利用了录音中 lots of people\ninterested in watching such movies 与 London 的搭配——但原文只说「有很多人感兴\n趣」，并未限定为「年轻伦敦人」；选项把宽泛的 lots of people 偷换成具体的 young\nLondoners，加了原文没有的修饰语 young。\n借用原文：lots of people interested in watching such movies\nC) 与原文相反\nThey are hard for English people to appreciate. 的极性和女方观点相反——女方一直在\n反驳「外语片在伦敦没人看」的怀疑，强调 there's enough demand 和 lots of people\ninterested，没有任何「难以欣赏」的意思。\n借用原文：there's enough demand", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn about Astrid Natley, a secondary school math teacher?", "options": ["A) She doesn't think smartphones can replace tablets.", "B) She incorporates smartphones into her teaching.", "C) She cannot stop children using smartphones.", "D) She regards smartphones as a distraction."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She incorporates smartphones into her teaching.\n【定位】Astrid Natley, a secondary school math teacher, incorporates phones into her classroom.\nAstrid Natley是一位中学数学老师，她把手机融入到了自己的课堂里。\n【信号】人物登场首句即定调。原文Astrid Natley, a secondary school math teacher,\nincorporates phones into her classroom.是该人物在文中首次出现的那句话，篇章独白介\n绍人物时通常\"姓名+身份+核心做法\"一句话点题，这种\"登场首句\"就是关于该人物事实的答\n案信号。\n【替换】incorporates phones into her classroom ↔ incorporates smartphones into her teaching\nphones=smartphones、classroom≈teaching\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里她说My school doesn't have money for tablets，意思是\"学校没钱买平板，所以\n才用手机\"，盗用了原文的tablets一词，做成\"她不认为手机能替代平板\"这种相反观点，属\n于原词陷阱+与原文相反。\n借用原文：My school doesn't have money for tablets\nC) 偷换概念\n录音If we stop children using phones, we're rejecting something they care about.\n是\"如果我们阻止孩子用手机，等于拒绝他们在乎的东西\"——是\"不愿阻止\"的价值判断；选\n项She cannot stop children using smartphones.盗用stop children using phones原\n词，却换成\"无法阻止\"，偷换了情态。\n借用原文：If we stop children using phones, we're rejecting something they care about.\nD) 与原文相反\n原文她把手机视为a powerful tool in their hands，是正面评价；选项She regards\nsmartphones as a distraction.却说她把手机视为\"干扰\"，立场与原文相反。\n借用原文：a powerful tool in their hands", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why does Helen Lockhart, a registered nurse, support a complete ban on smartphone use in the classroom?", "options": ["A) To enforce school discipline.", "B) To make students concentrate.", "C) To help children grow up to be professionals.", "D) To cultivate children's good study habits."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To help children grow up to be professionals.\n【定位】We need to teach children how to behave like the professionals they are striving to become.\n我们需要教孩子们像他们将来要努力成为的那些专业人士一样去行事。\n【信号】段尾\"need to\"目的陈述。原文We need to teach children how to behave like the\nprofessionals they are striving to become.位于Helen段落末尾，以We need to开头的句\n子是她整段论证的结论与目的——\"need to\"是表目的的强信号词，是答案信号。\n【替换】teach children how to behave like the professionals they are striving to become ↔\nTo help children grow up to be professionals\nbehave like professionals=grow up to be professionals\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音I will be subject to disciplinary procedure是她讲自己工作场所的纪律后果，作为引\n出\"教孩子像专业人士\"目的的铺垫；选项To enforce school discipline.盗用disciplinary原\n词，扭成\"她支持禁手机的目的是维护校纪\"，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I will be subject to disciplinary procedure\nB) 未提及\n录音里Helen段没有任何关于concentration/focus/attention的表述，选项To make\nstudents concentrate.是听上去合理但原文未提及的干扰项。\nD) 未提及\n录音里Helen段没有提到study habits或类似的\"养成学习习惯\"，选项To cultivate\nchildren's good study habits.是另一个\"听上去禁手机的合理理由\"，属于未提及陷阱。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What should students do in the classroom according to Richard Stone, an English teacher?", "options": ["A) Use books and pens only.", "B) Cut down their screen time.", "C) Make full use of electronic devices.", "D) Learn to use the internet for research."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Use books and pens only.\n【定位】so let's leave phones and laptops at home, and show them a different world in their lessons—\none of books and pens\n所以让我们把手机和笔记本电脑都留在家里，在课堂上向他们展示一个不一样的世界——一\n个由书本和钢笔构成的世界。\n【信号】let's+破折号定义结论句。原文so let's leave phones and laptops at home, and show\nthem a different world in their lessons—one of books and pens以\"so let's…\"建议式结\n构开头，又用破折号引出对\"a different world\"的同位语解释\"one of books and\npens\"——这是典型的\"结论+具象化\"信号，是答案句。\n【替换】leave phones and laptops at home ↔ Use books and pens only\n把手机和电脑留家=不用电子设备\nshow them a different world in their lessons—one of books and pens ↔ Use books and pens only\nbooks and pens的世界=只用书本和钢笔\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音Children are getting more than enough screen time in their lives without the\nneed to bring their phone into the classroom.提到screen time，是Richard用以解释\"为\n什么不该带手机进课堂\"的背景论据，并非他对学生课堂行为的主张；选项Cut down their\nscreen time.盗用screen time原词来制造听到就想选的诱惑。\n借用原文：Children are getting more than enough screen time in their lives without the need to\nbring their phone into the classroom.\nC) 与原文相反\nRichard明确主张let's leave phones and laptops at home，是反对在课堂用电子设备；选\n项Make full use of electronic devices.反过来说\"充分利用电子设备\"，立场反向。\n借用原文：let's leave phones and laptops at home\nD) 与原文相反\n录音The internet is too easy and too unreliable a research tool里，Richard明明在批\n评\"互联网作为研究工具太简单、太不可靠\"；选项Learn to use the internet for research.\n盗用internet、research原词，反过来说\"学会用网做研究\"，立场翻转。\n借用原文：The internet is too easy and too unreliable a research tool", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "How did Kate earn the reward from her mother?", "options": ["A) By cleaning the kitchen floor.", "B) By looking after her baby brother.", "C) By helping her mother do the dishes.", "D) By helping her brother wash windows."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By helping her brother wash windows.\n【定位】Kate, a six-year-old girl, helped her brother wash windows to get a reward promised by her\nmother, a purple doll that she wanted.\n凯特，一个六岁的小女孩，帮哥哥擦窗户，为的是拿到妈妈答应的奖励——一只她想要的紫\n色娃娃。\n【信号】to get a reward 的目的状语。helped her brother wash windows to get a reward\npromised by her mother 这句里 to get a reward 是目的状语，前面的动作（helped\nher brother wash windows）就是为了拿奖励而做的事，正是题目问的 how earn\nthe reward。\n【替换】helped her brother wash windows ↔ helping her brother wash windows\n几乎原词复现，仅时态变形\n【排除】A) 未提及\nBy cleaning the kitchen floor. 是常见家务但录音里完全没提，属于未提及型干扰，专骗那\n些把做家务挣奖励脑补成打扫卫生的考生。\nB) 原词陷阱\nBy looking after her baby brother. 盗用了录音里的 brother 一词制造熟感，但录音里说\n的是 helped her brother wash windows，是凯特帮哥哥，而不是照看弟弟。\n借用原文：helped her brother wash windows\nC) 原词陷阱\nBy helping her mother do the dishes. 蹭了录音里 helped 与为妈妈做事的氛围，但录音\n里凯特帮的是 brother、做的是 wash windows，不是帮 mother do the dishes。\n借用原文：promised by her mother", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did Kate do after her mother left the room?", "options": ["A) She ordered a large number of dolls.", "B) She checked if a purple doll was ordered.", "C) She mistakenly canceled her mom's order.", "D) She ordered a more expensive doll instead."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She ordered a large number of dolls.\n【定位】15 minutes and a few clicks later, with more than $350 worth of dolls in her cart, Kate placed\nthe order and ran off to play.\n15 分钟和几次点击之后，购物车里装着价值 350 多美元的娃娃，凯特下了单，跑去玩了。\n【信号】总结性结尾句加数量爆炸。15 minutes and a few clicks later, with more than $350\nworth of dolls in her cart, Kate placed the order 这句是这一段动作的收束句，350 美\n元、placed the order 几个反常细节直接交代了凯特在妈妈走后做了什么——她下了一大\n堆娃娃的订单。这种数量或价格爆表的细节，几乎一定是答案信号。\n【替换】more than $350 worth of dolls in her cart ↔ She ordered a large number of dolls.\n数量上的同义改写\nKate placed the order ↔ She ordered a large number of dolls.\n动词短语到单动词的等值替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nShe checked if a purple doll was ordered. 盗用了录音里 purple doll 和 check the\nshipping status 制造她检查了紫娃娃订单的幻觉，但录音里 check 的是 shipping status\n（物流），而且那是动作的起点不是后果，她做了什么的真正答案是后面的下单。\n借用原文：Kate asked if she could check the shipping status\nC) 张冠李戴\nShe mistakenly canceled her mom's order. 录音里恰恰是 cancel 这个词出现了，但执行\n者是妈妈第二天才取消的两单，不是凯特；凯特做的事是反方向的加单。\n借用原文：She was able to cancel two of them\nD) 偷换概念\nShe ordered a more expensive doll instead. 录音里凯特的问题不是换了一只更贵的，而\n是在原本一只之外又加了一大堆，重点是数量爆炸而不是单只价格升级，是过度推断式的偏\n移。\n借用原文：more than $350 worth of dolls in her cart", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What did Kate's family do after the packages arrived?", "options": ["A) They asked the children's hospital to pick them.", "B) They took all of them to the children's hospital.", "C) They opened all of them one by one.", "D) They discussed where to keep them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They took all of them to the children's hospital.\n【定位】Instead of sending back the dolls, her father suggested giving them to the local children's\nhospital. So the next week, Kate's family loaded up their car and took all the dolls to the\nplayroom at the children's hospital.\n她父亲提议不要把娃娃寄回去，而是捐给当地的儿童医院。于是第二周，凯特一家装了一\n车，把所有娃娃送到了儿童医院的活动室。\n【信号】结果句 So 加 took all the dolls。So the next week, Kate's family loaded up their car\nand took all the dolls to the playroom at the children's hospital. 中的 So 是因果结果\n信号，把前面父亲提议的话变成实际行动，直接给出全家把所有娃娃送去了医院这个确定\n答案。\n【替换】took all the dolls to the playroom at the children's hospital ↔\nThey took all of them to the children's hospital.\n几乎原句直译\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey asked the children's hospital to pick them. 蹭了录音里的 children's hospital 制造\n熟感，但录音里是家里人把娃娃送去，不是请医院来挑，动作方向完全相反。\n借用原文：took all the dolls to the playroom at the children's hospital\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey opened all of them one by one. 盗用了录音里 opened up all the boxes 这个动\n作，但执行人是凯特和医院里的小女孩患者们，不是凯特一家，而且发生地点也在医院里、\n不是包裹一到家就打开。\n借用原文：they opened up all the boxes together\nD) 未提及\ndiscussed where to keep them. 这个动作完全没出现，录音直奔父亲提议捐医院，没经过\n商量放哪儿。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "According to recent research conducted in Australia, what has a lasting impact on one's life?", "options": ["A) The time one starts school.", "B) The classroom atmosphere.", "C) The school that one attends.", "D) The relationship with classmates."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The time one starts school.\n【定位】The researchers found that the time when a person starts school has an enduring impact on\ntheir lives.\n研究者发现，一个人开始上学的时间会对其一生产生持久的影响。\n【信号】researchers found 这一研究结论信号。篇章独白里 The researchers found that 是典型\n的结论提示词——后面紧跟的句子就是研究的核心发现，几乎必然是答案句。再加上 has\nan enduring impact on their lives 与题干 lasting impact on one's life 完全对应，可以\n确认就是这一句。\n【替换】the time when a person starts school ↔ The time one starts school.\n句式压缩同义替换\nhas an enduring impact on their lives ↔ a lasting impact on one's life\n形容词同义替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音从头到尾没有出现 atmosphere、classroom environment 这类表达，The\nclassroom atmosphere. 属于教育话题里常用的凑数干扰，没有任何原文支撑。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe school that one attends. 借了录音里反复出现的 school 一词来制造熟悉感，但录音\n说的是 the time when a person starts school——重点在 time 不在 school 本身，把入\n学时间偷换成了上的什么学校，是典型的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the time when a person starts school\nD) 原词陷阱\nThe relationship with classmates. 提到的 classmates 虽然在录音里以 compared to\ntheir classmates 出现过一次，但录音只是用 classmates 来做参照对象，没有讨论同学关\n系本身对人生的影响，属于借词不传义。\n借用原文：compared to their classmates", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What was the purpose of the Australian research?", "options": ["A) To help parents decide when to send their children to school.", "B) To find causes for differences in the participants' performance.", "C) To identify students having potential to be professional athletes.", "D) To offer constructive suggestions for making educational policies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To find causes for differences in the participants' performance.\n【定位】But the Australian researchers wanted to discover what was driving these differences.\n但澳大利亚的研究者们想要弄清楚究竟是什么在驱动这些差异。\n【信号】but + wanted to discover 这一双重信号。But the Australian researchers wanted to\ndiscover what was driving these differences. 既有转折信号 but，又有目的结构 wanted\nto discover，两个信号叠加，几乎可以确认这就是研究目的的官方表述句。\n【替换】wanted to discover what was driving these differences ↔\nTo find causes for differences in the participants' performance.\n同义动词与名词改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nTo help parents decide when to send their children to school. 借了 starts school 的话\n题词，把研究目的偷换成给家长的择校建议，但录音从未提及 parents 或 advice，是典型\n的应用层偷换。\n借用原文：the time when a person starts school\nC) 原词陷阱\nTo identify students having potential to be professional athletes. 借了录音里\nprofessional athletes 一词来制造听到就想选的诱惑，但录音里 athletes 只是顺带列举的\n成功人士之一，不是研究目的。\n借用原文：professional athletes\nD) 过度推断\nTo offer constructive suggestions for making educational policies. 把研究目的拔高到\n教育政策层面，录音里完全没有出现 policy、policymaker 或 suggestion 等表达，属于无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Who were the participants in the researchers' first study?", "options": ["A) Political leaders.", "B) Financial analysts.", "C) Professional athletes.", "D) High-school students."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） High-school students.\n【定位】In the first study more than 600 high school students were surveyed about their tendency to\ntake risks and feel confident.\n在第一项研究中，超过六百名高中生接受了关于其冒险倾向与自信感的调查。\n【信号】In the first study 这一段落标志。In the first study 是典型的研究步骤标志词，后面紧跟\n的就是该研究的被试群体，是非常明确的定位信号；紧随其后的 high school students 就\n是答案。\n【替换】more than 600 high school students ↔ High-school students.\n数量修饰被剥离的概括式替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nPolitical leaders. 借了录音末尾 political leaders 一词制造熟悉感，但 political leaders 是\n在 including athletes, political leaders and entrepreneurs 这一举例结构里出现，是成功\n人士的例子，不是第一项研究的被试。\n借用原文：including athletes, political leaders and entrepreneurs\nB) 未提及\nFinancial analysts. 在录音里完全没有对应表达，录音只在 managing their finances 中出\n现过 finances 一词，但讲的是受试者管理财务时的冒险行为，与金融分析师这个职业身份\n毫不相关。\nC) 原词陷阱\nProfessional athletes. 借了录音里反复出现的 professional athletes 一词，但录音里\nathletes 始终是作为成功人士的例子出现，从未被列为第一项研究的被试群体，属于借词换\n位。\n借用原文：professional athletes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "According to the researchers, what is one characteristic of successful people?", "options": ["A) Leadership ability.", "B) Commitment to work.", "C) Risk-taking.", "D) Self-confidence."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Risk-taking.\n【定位】While risk-taking can be considered a negative attribute, it's also a characteristic of successful\npeople, including athletes, political leaders and entrepreneurs.\n尽管冒险有时被视为一种负面特质，但它同时也是成功人士的一种特征，包括运动员、政界\n领袖与企业家在内。\n【信号】while 让步 + it's also 转折强调。While risk-taking can be considered a negative\nattribute, it's also a characteristic of successful people 用 while 引导让步、随后 it's\nalso 强调真正想说的一面——这种让步+强调结构里的强调句几乎必然是答案。\n【替换】it's also a characteristic of successful people ↔ one characteristic of successful people\n句式结构高度复现\nrisk-taking ↔ Risk-taking.\n代词指代回填\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nLeadership ability. 利用了录音末尾 political leaders 一词制造领导力联想，但录音说成功\n人士包括政界领袖，并没有把 leadership ability 列为他们的共性特征。\n借用原文：including athletes, political leaders and entrepreneurs\nB) 未提及\nCommitment to work. 是非常常见的成功学描述，但录音里完全没有出现 commitment、\ndedication、hard work 等字眼，属于无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nSelf-confidence. 借了录音里多次出现的 self-confidence 与 feel confident，但录音只把\nself-confidence 作为研究的假设变量，最终结论强调的特征是 risk-taking，不是 self-\nconfidence。\n借用原文：it's also a characteristic of successful people", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) alike\n副词\n【词性槽】主语 + ___ + have dreamed，插在主语与谓语间修饰整句\n【依据】alike 作副词表\"同样地、都\"，强调三类人\"都\"做过此梦\n前文列举 Authors, artists and children三类主体，alike 置于其后表\"作家、艺术家和孩子\n都一样（地做过这样的梦）\"，呼应开篇\"延续数百年的幻想\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) struck\n动词\n【词性槽】it found itself ___ by an invasion，过去分词作宾补\n【依据】be struck by 表\"遭受、被袭击\"，小岛被彩蛋\"大军\"侵袭\n宾语是 an invasion of colored plastic eggs（彩蛋的\"入侵/大举涌来\"）。be struck by\n（遭…袭击）与 invasion 呼应，\"小岛发现自己被一场彩蛋入侵所袭\"，生动且搭配地道。\n【竞争词】G) overthrown overthrown 指\"推翻（政权）\"，对象错位\noverthrow 用于推翻政府、制度，海岛被彩蛋\"推翻\"语义荒谬，与 invasion of eggs 不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) delight\n名词\n【词性槽】to the ___ of 结构里，定冠词 the 后必跟名词\n【依据】蛋里有玩具，孩子\"非常高兴\"，且 much to the delight of 是固定表达\n下文给出原因 because the eggs contained toys——蛋里装着玩具，对孩子是大喜\n事。\"much to the delight of local children\"（令当地孩子大喜）语义与搭配双双吻合。\n【竞争词】F) quantity 语义不对，孩子的是\"喜悦\"不是\"数量\"\nquantity 指数量，\"令孩子非常数量的是\"不通；且 much to the quantity of 不成立。\nH) mess 感情色彩相反，mess 是后文市长用的贬义词\nmess（烂摊子）是负面的，而这里说的是孩子因蛋里有玩具而开心，色彩相反。mess 留给第\n33 空（this is a huge __(33)__）。\nB) wildlife 语义不搭，且 wildlife 留给第 34 空\n\"令当地孩子野生动物\"不通；wildlife 应填 cause serious problems for __(34)__。\nJ) human \"令孩子人类的\"语义不成立\nhuman 在此既不能表达孩子的情绪反应，也不与 much to the ... of 框架搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) suspect\n动词\n【词性槽】Police ___ the eggs came...，主语 Police 后缺谓语\n【依据】警方\"猜测\"蛋来自一艘失货货轮，suspect + 从句表推测\n下文 the eggs came from a freighter that lost part of its cargo是警方推断的内容；\nsuspect that... 正表\"警方怀疑/认为……\"，符合警方调查口吻。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) intense\n形容词\n【词性槽】an unusually ___ storm，副词 + 空 + 名词，空作定语修饰 storm\n【依据】形容\"风暴\"且后文\"自2006年以来最猛\"印证其强烈\n同句下文 the worst to hit Germany's northeastern coast since 2006——自 2006 年以\n来最严重的一场。an unusually intense storm（异常猛烈的风暴）与\"最严重\"互相印证。\n【竞争词】M) colorful 搭配荒谬，\"多彩的风暴\"不通，且 colorful 留给第 32 空\ncolorful 形容色彩，修饰 storm 不合常理；它对应 everything was so __(32)__说蛋的缤纷。\nK) effective \"有效的风暴\"语义不成立\neffective 多修饰方法、手段，与 storm 不搭。\nE) similar \"相似的风暴\"无所指，且缺被比较对象\nsimilar 需有比较对象，且\"异常相似的风暴\"逻辑空洞，与\"最严重\"的语境不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) wore\n动词\n【词性槽】the joy ___ off soon，主语 joy 后缺谓语，紧跟 off\n【依据】wear off 是固定短语\"逐渐消退\"，喜悦\"很快消退\"合转折\n前句 \"The surprise eggs have found their way to freedom\"是欢快的，紧接 However,\nthe joy of the moment __(31)__ off soon——However 转折，\"那一刻的喜悦很快消\n退\"。wore off（wear off 过去式）正表\"渐渐消失\"。\n【竞争词】D) struck strike off 不表\"消退\"，与 joy 搭配不当\nstruck 与 off 不构成\"喜悦消退\"之义，且语义上\"喜悦击落\"不通；wear off 才是\"（感觉）逐渐\n消失\"的地道搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) colorful\n形容词\n【词性槽】everything was so ___，系动词 was + so + 空作表语\n【依据】回指前文\"彩色塑料蛋\"，市长起初觉得\"一切如此缤纷\"像奇迹\n前文 an invasion of colored plastic eggs——彩色塑料蛋遍地。市长说 At first I thought\nthis was a wonder, because everything was so __(32)__，\"起初以为是奇迹，因为一切都\n那么缤纷\"，colorful 正回指那些彩蛋。\n【竞争词】K) effective \"一切都那么有效\"与\"奇迹\"语境不搭\neffective 形容功效，无法解释为何\"像个奇迹\"，也不回指彩蛋画面。\nE) similar \"一切都那么相似\"语义突兀，缺比较对象\nsimilar 需比较对象，\"so similar\"在\"奇迹\"的惊叹语境里说不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) mess\n名词\n【词性槽】a huge ___，冠词 a + 形容词 huge + 空，空作中心名词\n【依据】but then 转折后揭示是个\"大麻烦/烂摊子\"\n句中 but then we realized that this is a huge __(33)__ in the end——\"但随后我们意识\n到这最终是个大麻烦\"。but then 与前半\"以为是奇迹\"对立，需一个负面名词，mess（烂摊\n子）正合。\n【竞争词】F) quantity 中性词，撑不起 but then 的负面转折\na huge quantity（大量）只表数量，无负面色彩，无法与\"以为是奇迹\"形成\"喜→忧\"的对立。\nJ) human \"一个巨大的人类\"语义不通\nhuman 作可数名词指\"一个人\"，\"a huge human\"在此荒谬。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— quantity 也是名词、a huge quantity 看着也通，易忽略\n色彩。this is a huge __(33)__ in the end 中 a huge quantity（大量）语法通顺，易被\n选；防坑：but then 要求负面转折，quantity 中性、无法与前半\"奇迹\"对立，必须用贬义\n的 mess。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) wildlife\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 for 后接宾语，需名词\n【依据】塑料袋等垃圾对\"野生动物\"造成危害，环保语境最贴\n上文 the plastic bags and other materials that have washed ashore——冲上岸的塑料\n袋等垃圾。海洋塑料垃圾危害的正是 wildlife（野生动物），逻辑顺理成章。\n【竞争词】J) human 义近干扰：垃圾确也害人，但本句焦点是海岛生态而非人\nhuman 看着也沾边，但塑料垃圾在环保叙事里的典型受害者是野生动物；且全文未展开\"对人的\n危害\"，选 wildlife 更贴文本，不靠常识硬推。\nF) quantity \"对数量造成问题\"语义不通\nproblems for quantity 不成立，quantity 不是危害的承受方。\nH) mess \"对烂摊子造成问题\"逻辑不通\nmess 不是被危害的对象；且它将用于第 33 空。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— human 词性也对、常识上\"垃圾害人\"也通，最易误选。\ncause serious problems for __(34)__ 里 human 与 wildlife 语法皆可、生活常识还偏\n向\"害人\"；防坑：紧扣本段海岛环保焦点，文本说的是塑料垃圾对野生动物的危害，选贴文\n本的 wildlife，不靠常识硬推。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) wore", "B) wildlife", "C) suspect", "D) struck", "E) similar", "F) quantity", "G) overthrown", "H) mess", "I) intense", "J) human", "K) effective", "L) delight", "M) colorful", "N) bore", "O) alike"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) bore\n动词\n【词性槽】They ___ notes...，主语 They 后缺谓语，带宾语 notes\n【依据】蛋\"带有/装着\"远方的纸条，bore（bear 过去式）表携带\n上文 all these little eggs contained an extra treat with their toys说蛋里还藏着额外惊\n喜。承接此意，They bore notes from afar——\"它们带着来自远方的纸条\"，bore 取\nbear\"携带、装载\"义，与 contained 呼应。\n【竞争词】A) wore wore 指\"穿戴\"，蛋\"穿着纸条\"不通\nwore（wear 过去式）用于穿衣戴饰，与 notes 不搭；它对应第 31 空的 wore off。\nD) struck struck 是\"击中\"，\"击中纸条\"语义荒谬\nstrike 表击打、袭击，不能与 notes 构成\"带有纸条\"之义。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Many people today still find name hyphenation upon marriage unacceptable.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】连字符姓不被接受\n【定位】If you care about outside opinions on your name, you should know that a large portion of\ntoday's society is annoyed by the hyphenated name.\n如果你在意别人对你名字的看法，你应该知道，当今社会有很大一部分人反感连字符姓。\n【改写】Many people today still find name hyphenation upon marriage unacceptable ↔\na large portion of today's society is annoyed by the hyphenated name\n整句同义改写\n【辨邻】 B B 段 The act of keeping her own last name was considered taboo\\(禁忌\\) 讲的是\n过去对\"保留娘家姓\"的不接受，既非\"今天\"也非\"连字符姓\"，故不是 Q36 的源头。\nOnce upon a time, it was just customary for a bride to take her groom's last name", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "As a compromise, a bride will in most cases adopt a name that combines the couple's last names.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】折中：合并两姓\n【定位】Most of the time, though, the most popular compromise is to hyphenate your last name and\nthe last name of your groom.\n不过大多数情况下，最常见的折中办法是把你的姓和新郎的姓用连字符连起来。\n【改写】a name that combines the couple's last names ↔\nhyphenate your last name and the last name of your groom\n上位词对具体操作\nin most cases ↔ the most popular compromise\n程度词对应\n【辨邻】 J J 段 The hyphenation is the epitome\\(体现\\) of a compromise 讲的是\"连姓为何算\n折中\"，并未陈述\"采用合并两姓的名字\"这一做法，故非 Q37 源头。\nHyphenating the two names is a way for each person to, at least a little bit, \"win\" the argument", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The bride should consider adopting her groom's last name whether he feels strongly about it or not.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】新郎在意与否都要考虑随夫姓\n【定位】Whether your future husband insisting on your adopting his last name is a red flag to you or\nnot, it is still something that you should take into consideration.\n无论你未来的丈夫坚持要你随他的姓是不是一个危险信号，这都是你应该纳入考量的事情。\n【改写】should consider adopting her groom's last name whether he feels strongly about it or not ↔\nWhether your future husband insisting on your adopting his last name is a red flag to you or\nnot, it is still something that you should take into consideration\n让步句式重构", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Making preparations for marriage causes a lot of stress.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】筹婚带来压力\n【定位】While being married is great and wonderful, the act of getting married can be quite stressful.\n虽然婚姻本身美好幸福，但结婚这件事本身可能相当让人有压力。\n【改写】Making preparations for marriage ↔\nthe planning of the ceremony itself, the merging\\(合并\\) of two households\n概括对具体\ncauses a lot of stress ↔ the act of getting married can be quite stressful\n同根词变形\n【辨邻】 K K 段 It's easy to get overwhelmed just thinking about it 的发怵对象是 legally\nchange your name all over the place（到处改名），不是 Q39 说的婚礼筹备。\nYou'll need to change your name at the bank, with social security, with your credit cards", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Hyphenating the last names could be a win-win solution should arguments arise about what name to adopt upon marriage.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】连姓是双赢方案\n【定位】Hyphenating the two names is a way for each person to, at least a little bit, \"win\"\nthe argument.\n把两个姓连起来，是让每个人都能在争论中至少赢上一点的办法。\n【改写】a win-win solution ↔ a way for each person to, at least a little bit, \"win\" the argument\n复合词摊开\nshould arguments arise ↔ \"win\" the argument\n同词 argument 呼应\n【辨邻】 D D 段 the most popular compromise is to hyphenate 是介绍操作，未出\n现\"赢\"或\"争论\"，不承载 Q40 的\"双赢\"语义。\nFor example, if your groom's name is John Smith and your name is Kate Jones", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It used to be considered socially unacceptable for a bride to retain her maiden name.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】过去保留娘家姓不被接受\n【定位】The act of keeping her own last name was considered taboo(禁忌) and people's eyebrows\nwould raise right off their faces when they found out that the bride was even considering\nsomething so radical.\n保留自己姓氏的做法曾被视为禁忌，当人们发现新娘竟然在考虑如此激进的事时，会惊讶得\n眉毛都快飞出脸了。\n【改写】It used to be considered socially unacceptable ↔ was considered taboo\\(禁忌\\)\n同义改写\nfor a bride to retain her maiden name ↔ The act of keeping her own last name\n近义短语替换\n【辨邻】 H H 段 a large portion of today's society is annoyed by the hyphenated name 讲\n的是\"今天对连字符姓\"的反感，与 Q41\"过去对娘家姓\"不接受时间、对象皆异。\nSome people find it \"snobby\"\\(势利的\\)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The bride who adopts a hyphenated last name after marriage can maintain connections with their past achievements.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】保住过往成就\n【定位】It allows you to stay connected to accomplishments that you achieved before you got married.\n它能让你与婚前取得的成就保持联系。\n【改写】maintain connections with their past achievements ↔\nstay connected to accomplishments that you achieved before you got married\n成对近义替换\n【辨邻】 G G 段 your name is associated with the identity you've built up 落在\"身份\"上，未\n提学历、出版物等具体成就，故非 Q42 源头。\nYou've spent your whole life building your identity under a certain name", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Hyphenating names allows the bride to preserve her own identity while respecting tradition.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】既保身份又尊重传统\n【定位】your name is associated with the identity you've built up and hyphenation allows you to\nrespect that while also respecting tradition and your husband's family's identity.\n你的名字与你建立起来的身份相关联，连字符姓让你既能尊重这份身份，又能尊重传统以及\n丈夫家庭的身份。\n【改写】preserve her own identity ↔ respect that\n近义动词替换\nwhile respecting tradition ↔ while also respecting tradition\n原词复现\n【辨邻】 E E 段重在 stay connected to accomplishments（成就），未出现\"尊重传统\"，与\nQ43\"既保身份又尊重传统\"的双重诉求不符。\nIt is a way of you keeping your own identity while also making your future husband happy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "No matter what name the bride adopts, it is most important that the newlyweds truly love each other.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】相爱最重要\n【定位】At the end of the day, whether you each keep your names, whether you come up with\nan entirely new name for the two of you to share or whether you hyphenate your current\nlast names, what matters is that you love each other and are going to be joining your\nlives together.\n说到底，无论你们各自保留姓氏、还是另取一个两人共用的新姓、抑或把现有的姓连起来，\n重要的是你们彼此相爱、即将携手共度人生。\n【改写】No matter what name the bride adopts ↔\nwhether you each keep your names, whether you come up with an entirely new name ... or\nwhether you hyphenate your current last names\n概括对穷举\nit is most important that the newlyweds truly love each other ↔\nwhat matters is that you love each other\n同义改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Legally speaking, the bride is free to choose whatever name she prefers.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】法律上随便选名都行\n【定位】Keeping your name and joining it to your husband's through hyphenation is as legal as simply\nadopting his name or as simply keeping your own and leaving his out of the picture entirely.\n把你的姓通过连字符与丈夫的姓连起来，和单纯随夫姓、或单纯只保留自己的姓而完全不要\n丈夫的姓，在法律上是一样合法的。\n【改写】Legally speaking ↔ is as legal as\n同根词对应\nthe bride is free to choose whatever name she prefers ↔\nKeeping your name and joining it to your husband's through hyphenation is as legal as simply\nadopting his name or as simply keeping your own\n并列等价推出自由\n【辨邻】 K K 段 you are going to have to legally change your name all over the place 落\n在\"办理改名手续\"上，未论\"各种姓都合法、可自由选\"，故非 Q45 源头。\nYou'll need to change your name at the bank, with social security", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about the brightest people?", "options": ["A) They can make silly mistakes in straightforward situations.", "B) They usually turn a blind eye to their own weaknesses.", "C) They are admired by people around them.", "D) They can differ in their personalities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the passage about the brightest people? 是开放式问 \"关\n于聪明人我们知道了什么\"，没有 infer/imply 之类的推断信号，也不问主旨或态度——属\n于覆盖全篇的细节判断题。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the passage about the brightest people? 主干是 what do we\nlearn，about the brightest people 是后置介词短语修饰 learn 的内容范围；结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The brightest people and strongest leaders sometimes make errors others don't, especially in\nsituations that require common sense.\n最聪明的人和最强的领导者有时会犯别人不会犯的错，尤其是在那些需要常识的场合。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmake errors others don't, especially in situations that require common sense ↔\nmake silly mistakes in straightforward situations\nThey can make silly mistakes in straightforward situations. 把预判的 \"在常识场景中\n犯错\" 完整复现，且与定位句逐成分对应，是定位句的同义替换。\nB) 无中生有\n文章只说聪明人有 blind spots in how they use logic，并没有说他们对 \"自己的弱点\n视而不见\"——turn a blind eye to their own weaknesses 是凭文字面感受瞎想出来的\n关联。\nC) 无中生有\n文章只提到 intelligent people earn more money, accumulate more wealth, and\neven live longer，没说他们被人 \"敬仰\"——admired by people around them 属原文\n未提的事实陈述。\nD) 答非所问\n末段确实提到 Smart people develop overachieving personalities，但全文聚焦的是\n聪明人的共性问题，并未论述他们 \"个性各异\"——differ in their personalities 与文章\n问的 \"我们了解到什么\" 不在一个频道。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What accounts for the existence of intelligent people's logical blind spots?", "options": ["A) Their irrational way of thinking.", "B) Too much faith in their ability to think.", "C) Their ignorance of behavioural science.", "D) Too much concern about their work"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What accounts for the existence of intelligent people's logical blind spots? 指向\n一个具体概念（逻辑盲点）的成因，是回到具体段落找因果句的细节题。\n【拆句】What accounts for the existence of intelligent people's logical blind spots? 主干\nwhat accounts for X，X 是 the existence of intelligent people's logical blind spots\n（名词化结构，相当于问 \"为什么聪明人会存在逻辑盲点\"），把名词化拆回动词理解即可。\n【定位】Intelligent people are more prone to silly mistakes because of blind spots in how they\nuse logic. These blind spots exist because smart people tend to be overconfident in their\nreasoning abilities.\n聪明人更容易犯傻错，是因为他们运用逻辑时存在盲点。这些盲点之所以存在，是因为聪明\n人倾向于对自己的推理能力过度自信。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说聪明人 Rational thinking and intelligence don't tend to go hand in hand\n——意思是聪明人未必理性，但这并不等于他们 \"思维不理性\" 就是盲点的成因；真正的\n成因是过度自信。irrational way of thinking 把伴生现象当成因，属于因果倒置。\nB) ✓ 正确\noverconfident in their reasoning abilities ↔ Too much faith in their ability to think\nToo much faith in their ability to think. 把原文 \"对推理能力过度自信\" 的核心搬过\n来，且 faith / ability to think 两个成分都能在定位句里逐一着落。\nC) 无中生有\nignorance of behavioural science 把 \"行为科学\" 这种学科概念引进来——但文章自始\n至终没有提行为科学，是凭空捏造的成因。\nD) 无中生有\n文章没有说聪明人 \"过度关心工作\"——Too much concern about their work 是凭日常\n对 \"聪明人=工作狂\" 的刻板印象瞎编出来的。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How do smart people react when they are found to be wrong?", "options": ["A) They shift the blame to others.", "B) They graciously accept the facts.", "C) They may feel shocked.", "D) They may get offended."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do smart people react when they are found to be wrong? 限定了具体情境\n\"被发现错了\"，问对应反应，是定位到具体段落求证的细节题。\n【拆句】How do smart people react when they are found to be wrong? 主句 How do smart\npeople react，when 引导时间状语从句限定情境；从句用被动语态 are found to be\nwrong（被发现错了），主体仍是 smart people。\n【定位】For smart people, being wrong can feel like a personal attack, and being right, a necessity.\n对聪明人来说，被认定为错可以感觉像一次人身攻击，而正确则是一种必需。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文没有说聪明人会 \"把责任推给别人\"——shift the blame to others 是凭 \"聪明人不\n愿承认错\" 这一印象想象出来的下游行为。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说 It's hard for anyone to graciously accept the fact that they're wrong.\nIt's even harder for smart people——聪明人尤其难以坦然接受。graciously accept\nthe facts 直接照搬原文短语却把否定关系反过来，是典型反向陷阱。\nC) 偷换概念\nfeel shocked 把 \"被冒犯/受攻击感\" 偷换成 \"震惊\"——shocked 强调对事实意外，\noffended 强调对自尊受损的反应，两种情绪不同。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbeing wrong can feel like a personal attack ↔ They may get offended.\nThey may get offended. 把比喻 a personal attack 翻译成 get offended，是情绪反\n应的同义动词化，且 may 与原文 can 在语气上一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What may happen to smart people who find it difficult to accept suggestions?", "options": ["A) They may suffer in their professional and private life.", "B) They may lose faith in their administrative abilities.", "C) They may commit more errors than ever before.", "D) They may experience a lot of emotional stress."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What may happen to smart people who find it difficult to accept suggestions?\n问 \"对接受不了建议的聪明人会发生什么\"，是对特定情境后果的细节求证。\n【拆句】What may happen to smart people who find it difficult to accept suggestions? 主干\n是 what may happen to smart people；who find it difficult to accept suggestions 是\n修饰 smart people 的定语从句，限定了 \"哪一类聪明人\"。\n【定位】Not only does this tendency hinder their growth and performance, it can lead to harmful\nrelationships, both personally and professionally.\n这种倾向不仅会阻碍他们的成长与表现，还可能导致有害的人际关系——无论是私人层面还\n是职业层面。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nharmful relationships, both personally and professionally ↔\nsuffer in their professional and private life\nsuffer in their professional and private life 既覆盖了原文 \"professionally\" 又覆盖了\n\"personally\"——两个并列限定都能落地，是定位句的完整同义改写。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说 hinder their growth and performance，是 \"阻碍成长与表现\"，并未说他们\n\"对自己的管理能力失去信心\"——lose faith in their administrative abilities 把 \"成长\n与表现受阻\" 偷换成 \"对管理能力失去信心\"。\nC) 无中生有\n原文没有把 \"接受不了反馈\" 与 \"犯更多错\" 直接挂钩——commit more errors than\never before 是把第一段的 errors 概念硬拉到第五段后果中。\nD) 无中生有\nexperience a lot of emotional stress 把后果限定为 \"情绪压力\"——但原文说的是 \"成\n长与表现受阻 + 关系有害\"，从未提及情绪压力。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is said about those working with or under overachieving people?", "options": ["A) They put a lot of effort into their work.", "B) They set higher goals for themselves.", "C) They are under increasing pressure.", "D) They take less time to get things done."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is said about those working with or under overachieving people? 问 \"关于\n和聪明人共事的人，文章说了什么\"，是末段定位的细节题。\n【拆句】What is said about those working with or under overachieving people? 主干 What is\nsaid about X；X 是 those working with or under overachieving people，其中\nworking with or under overachieving people 是分词短语后置修饰 those，表示 \"那些与\n过分追求成就的人共事或在其手下做事的人\"。注意 with or under 是并列介词，对象都是\noverachieving people。\n【定位】They set the bar too high, and when people take too long or don't get things quite right, they\nassume it's due to a lack of effort. So they push even harder.\n他们把标杆定得太高，当别人花的时间过长或事情做得不够准确时，他们就认定这是因为对\n方努力不够，于是施压得更狠。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说聪明人 assume it's due to a lack of effort——是聪明人 \"以为\" 别人不努力，\n并不是别人真的努力。put a lot of effort into their work 把聪明人的 \"以为\" 当成事\n实，恰好与原文意涵相反。\nB) 张冠李戴\nset higher goals for themselves 这一行为在原文里属于聪明人——They set the bar\ntoo high，主语是聪明人；选项却把它安在 \"和聪明人共事的人\" 头上，是把行为主体张\n冠李戴。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthey push people too hard ↔ They are under increasing pressure.\nSo they push even harder. ↔ increasing pressure\nThey are under increasing pressure. 既覆盖 push people 的施压主轴（变成 under\npressure），又覆盖 push even harder 的加码动态（变成 increasing），两个成分都\n能在原文找到落点。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文说聪明人 when people take too long——意思是这些人花的时间 \"过长\"，而\ntake less time to get things done 把 \"过长\" 反成 \"更短\"，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why is the U.S. State Department launching an online learning program for refugees?", "options": ["A) To enable them to settle down in host countries.", "B) To allow them to learn as many job skills as possible.", "C) To offer them a platform to pursue advanced degrees.", "D) To help them to meet new employment standards."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why is the U.S. State Department launching an online learning program for\nrefugees?问的是一个具体的发起原因，答案应能在原文中明确找到对应句。\n【拆句】Why is the U.S. State Department launching an online learning program for\nrefugees?是一个 Why 引导的现在进行时疑问句，主语是机构名，介词短语for refugees点\n明服务对象，结构平铺、无陷阱。\n【定位】Refugees who settle in other countries often find themselves unable to continue on their\nprevious career path due to a new set of employer standards or skills requirements. To solve\nthis problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution: online learning.\n在他国安顿的难民常常发现，自己因雇主新一套的标准或技能要求而无法延续原来的职业路\n径。为解决这个问题，美国国务院正在推出一个方案：在线学习。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：把前提说成目的\nTo enable them to settle down in host countries.把项目目的说成帮助难民「在东道\n国安顿下来」，但原文中settle in other countries是难民已经发生的状态、是项目的前\n提，不是项目的目的。\nB) 过度推断：拔高程度词\nTo allow them to learn as many job skills as possible.把目的扩成「学尽可能多的工\n作技能」，而原文目的是「应对一套新的雇主标准」，原文并没有用「尽可能多」这类\n极端量词。\nC) 偷换概念：证书↔学位\nTo offer them a platform to pursue advanced degrees.把目的说成「追求高等学\n位」，但原文第二段说的是obtain professional certificates，是专业证书而非学位。\nD) ✓ 正确\na new set of employer standards or skills requirements ↔ new employment standards\nTo solve this problem, the U.S. State Department is posing a solution ↔ To help them to meet\n预判答案是「帮助难民应对他国雇主的新标准」，与To help them to meet new\nemployment standards.在主语、动作、对象、目的状语上四点全部对应，是唯一与定\n位句构成同义替换的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we know about \"Coursera for Refugees\"?", "options": ["A) It provides refugees with a wide range of courses free of charge.", "B) It offers free online data services for refugees around the world.", "C) It allows refugees to obtain degrees in various academic fields.", "D) It supplies refugees with a great variety of job opportunities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we know about \"Coursera for Refugees\"?要求选出关于该项目可以确认的事\n实，属于客观信息核对。\n【拆句】What do we know about \"Coursera for Refugees\"?结构非常简单，主句是What do we\nknow，介词宾语就是引号里的专名，无干扰修饰。\n【定位】They can apply for fully funded access to Coursera's course catalog, which means they can\ntake all of the platform's classes and obtain professional certificates for free. The platform\ncurrently offers professor-led lectures on a broad range of topics, from data science to\nfashion design.\n他们可以申请由项目全额资助的Coursera课程目录访问权限，这意味着他们可以免费修读该\n平台的所有课程并获得专业证书。该平台目前提供由教授主讲的讲座，主题广泛，从数据科\n学到时装设计。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na broad range of topics ↔ a wide range of courses\nfor free ↔ free of charge\n预判要求「免费+各种各样的课」，It provides refugees with a wide range of\ncourses free of charge.在主语、动作、对象、范围、付费方式上五点齐备，是定位句\n最干净的同义复述。\nB) 偷换概念：把举例项当成核心\nIt offers free online data services for refugees around the world.把项目说成「提供\n免费在线数据服务」，但原文from data science to fashion design中的 data science\n只是众多课程主题之一，不是项目所提供的服务内容。\nC) 偷换概念：证书↔学位再现\nIt allows refugees to obtain degrees in various academic fields.说项目能让难民拿\n到「各学术领域的学位」，但原文是professional certificates——专业证书而非学位，\n也没有强调「学术领域」。\nD) 无中生有：用近义类比制造跳跃\nIt supplies refugees with a great variety of job opportunities.把项目说成提供「多\n种多样的工作机会」，但原文从头到尾说的是「课程」与「证书」，不是「工作」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the passage say is a consequence of the global migrant crisis?", "options": ["A) Instability increases in host countries.", "B) The global economic crisis intensifies.", "C) A lot more refugees die during migration.", "D) Refugees find it more difficult to get a job."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say is a consequence of the global migrant crisis?直接问「文\n章说……的后果是什么」，答案应能在原文明确找到。\n【拆句】What does the passage say is a consequence of the global migrant crisis?是\n「What+助动词+主语+谓语+从句宾语」的疑问句，从句is a consequence of...提示答案\n的逻辑位置是后果一端，结构平铺。\n【定位】As the world migrant crisis intensifies, employment is becoming a global concern.\n随着全球移民危机加剧，就业正成为全球性的关切。\n【选项】A) 过度推断：从一点滑向全局\nInstability increases in host countries.说东道国整体不稳定上升，但原文只说就业一\n项成为全球关切，并未提社会稳定层面。\nB) 张冠李戴：动词从一个主语挪到另一个主语\nThe global economic crisis intensifies.说全球经济危机加剧，但原文只说就业问题，\n不是「经济危机」；intensifies 在原文里是用来形容 migrant crisis 的，不是\neconomic crisis。\nC) 无中生有：用常识联想替代文本\nA lot more refugees die during migration.说更多难民死于迁徙途中，但原文从头到\n尾没有涉及死亡话题，属典型无中生有。\nD) ✓ 正确\nemployment is becoming a global concern ↔ Refugees find it more difficult to get a job\na global concern ↔ more difficult\n预判结论是「就业难度上升」，本篇主体始终是难民，Refugees find it more difficult\nto get a job.既保留了主体，又把抽象的「关切」落地到「找工作变难」，是唯一与定\n位句因果直接对应的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say causes refugees' difficulty in taking online courses?", "options": ["A) Cultural environment.", "B) Changed academic requirements.", "C) Lack of language skills.", "D) Difference in teaching platforms."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say causes refugees' difficulty in taking online courses?问的是\n「作者说」的具体原因，回原文找归因句即可。\n【拆句】What does the author say causes refugees' difficulty in taking online courses?主干是\nWhat causes ... difficulty，does the author say是插入语，in taking online courses做后\n置定语限定 difficulty，结构稍绕但无陷阱。\n【定位】For another, most of the classes on Coursera are in English, and though there are plans to add\ntranslations, language barriers can be a big obstacle.\n另一方面，Coursera上的大多数课程都是英文授课，尽管有添加翻译的计划，语言障碍仍可\n能是一个大问题。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：用相关话题替换原文话题\nCultural environment.把困难归因到「文化环境」，但原文第四段从未涉及文化差异，\n是无中生有。\nB) 张冠李戴：把另一困难的原因装到本题\nChanged academic requirements.想说的是「学术要求改变」，但原文can't simply\nrely on an online lecture to get them up-to-date on standards, policies, and\npractices说的是行业「标准、政策、做法」的更新，而非学术要求；且这是第一条困\n难，不是题54问的「上网课的困难原因」，而是「换行业的困难」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlanguage barriers can be a big obstacle ↔ Lack of language skills\nmost of the classes on Coursera are in English ↔ Lack of language skills\n预判明确指向「语言」，四个选项中只有Lack of language skills.提及语言；与定位句\n构成最干净的因果对应。\nD) 偷换概念：把对一物的质疑换成多物之差异\nDifference in teaching platforms.说困难是「教学平台之间的差异」，但原文there\nare the questions over the value of massive open online courses (MOOCs)\nthemselves谈的是对MOOCs整体价值的质疑，不是不同平台之间的差异。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-03-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What did the joint study find about the MOOCs several years ago?", "options": ["A) They helped solve learners' financial problems.", "B) They benefited most of the learners.", "C) They changed some learners' career path.", "D) They provided the most needed vocational skills."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did the joint study find about the MOOCs several years ago?问一个具体研究的\n具体发现，是直白的事实核对。\n【拆句】What did the joint study find about the MOOCs several years ago?是过去时一般疑问\n句，主语the joint study，宾语为 what，时间状语several years ago提示要找过去时句\n子，结构平铺。\n【定位】In 2015, a joint study found 72% of people taking the MOOCs saw career\nbenefits afterwards.\n2015年，一项联合研究发现，参加MOOCs课程的人中有72%在之后获得了职业上的益处。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念：用常识把「职业」换成「财务」\nThey helped solve learners' financial problems.说MOOCs帮学习者解决「财务问\n题」，但原文只说career benefits——职业益处，不是钱。\nB) ✓ 正确\n72% of people taking the MOOCs ↔ most of the learners\nsaw career benefits afterwards ↔ benefited\n预判结论是「大多数学习者获益」，They benefited most of the learners.在主语、动\n词、对象上三点对应——只是用程度词代替数字、用动词代替名词短语，是最干净的同\n义改写。\nC) 过度推断：把益处程度拔高到根本改变\nThey changed some learners' career path.说MOOCs「改变了某些学习者的职业路\n径」，但原文只说saw career benefits——获得职业益处，从「获益」到「改变路径」\n是过度推断。\nD) 过度推断+无中生有：加最高级、加具体内容\nThey provided the most needed vocational skills.说MOOCs提供「最需要的职业技\n能」，但原文只笼统说「职业益处」，并未说提供了什么具体技能，更没有「最需要」\n这种最高级。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
